<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mystras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mistras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mystras is a ruined medieval town on a spur of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-taygetus">Mount Taygetus</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> built a castle there in 1249; ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1262, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a> and a centre of late Byzantine art and learning, home of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">Gemistos Plethon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1460. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mystras.jpg" alt="Mystras" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9C%CF%85%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82,_Mystras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mystras stands on a slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taygetus">Taygetos</a> mountains above ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, among pines and cypresses. The steep ground shaped its plan, with distinct upper and lower city zones, and many streets are footpaths. The Palace of the Despots occupies the main terrace; its last main building follows the form of the Tekfur saray in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, with three storeys and a large audience hall on top.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In late 1248 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a>, ruler of the Frankish Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> and completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>. Near his residence of Lacedaemon, on a spur of Mount Taygetos, he built the fortress that became known as Mystras. In September 1259 he was defeated and captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> by the Nicaean emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, and was freed in exchange for an oath of fealty and the cession of Monemvasia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a> and Mystras. The handover took place in 1262. The people of Lacedaemon later abandoned their exposed city and settled in a new town below the fortress.</p><p>In 1349 the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> for his son, the Despot Manuel Kantakouzenos, and from then until 1460 Mystras was the residence of a despot. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a> took the Morea after Manuel's death in 1380, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-palaiologos">Theodore I Palaiologos</a>, despot from 1383 to 1407, recognised Ottoman suzerainty. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">Gemistos Plethon</a> lived at Mystras until his death in 1452 and accompanied the emperor John VIII Palaiologos to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1439. The last Byzantine emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI Palaiologos</a>, was despot there before he took the throne.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrios-palaiologos">Demetrios Palaiologos</a>, the last despot, surrendered the city to Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1460. The Venetians held it from 1687 to 1715, and otherwise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> held it until 1821. It was looted in 1770 after joining the Orlov revolt, and in 1825 Egyptian forces under Ibrahim massacred its population. The town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> was built about 9 km away in 1831.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins, including the fortress, palace, churches and monasteries, were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1989. Many of the most important churches still stand, among them St Demetrios, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>, St George and the Monastery of Peribleptos, and some of their frescoes survive. The only residents are nuns at the Pantanassa Monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mystras” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey’s younger son William II of Villehardouin completed the conquest by taking Monemvasia in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of <b>Mistras</b>, Grand Magne and Beaufort to control the Mani and the Slavic Melingoi of the Taygetus range.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At the fortress of Saint George in Skorta, the forces of the Byzantine governor of <b>Mystras</b>, Andronikos Asen, ambushed and defeated those of the Latin Principality of Achaea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Principality ceded <b>Mistras</b> and other forts to Byzantium in 1262, Byzantine forces pushed north.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-kalavryta">Barony of Kalavryta</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1320 Andronikos Asen, the Byzantine governor of <b>Mistras</b>, took Karytaina and much of Skorta, ending Frankish rule there.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li><li><blockquote>After William II was captured at Pelagonia in 1259, he ceded <b>Mistras</b>, Monemvasia and Grand Magne to Byzantium, and Passavant was lost to the Byzantines soon after, around 1263.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-passavant">Barony of Passavant</a></li><li><blockquote>Byzantium regained <b>Mistras</b>, Monemvasia and the Mani in 1262 from the Principality of Achaea and slowly expanded from there.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a></li></ul></section>
